warning this video contains spoilers for  The Last of Us season 1 episode 1 and  the entire Last of Us Part One game by  most accounts the premiere of HBO's The  Last of Us was a slam dunk in the eyes  of longtime fans of the game but what  crucial detail from the game got left  out the last of us Creator Neil druckman  told nerdreactor that the priority of  the game's story was to explore the  relationship between its two Central  characters Ellie and Joel druckman and  his team wanted to see if they could  successfully convince players that two  characters who are strangers to one  another at the start of the game could  grow to care for one another immensely  by the end thanks to the immersive  gameplay and the intense narrative the  writers inflict upon the pair they  succeeded by all accounts without the  interactive aspect of gameplay to rope  viewers in the last of us showrunners  were always going to need to make  significant changes to achieve the same  effect to druckman and series co-creator  Craig Mason's credit many of the changes  showcased in the premiere all but prove  that no one besides them could craft an  adaptation so lovingly faithful in its  deviation and EXP Mansion however  certain changes we witness in the pilot  could threaten the Integrity of the  project altogether despite the game's  heavy Reliance on relationships it seems  that the writers of the show decided to  heavily alter one of the central opening  plot points the relationship between  Ellie and Marlene while their friendship  may seem inconsequential to what Joel  and Ellie developed throughout the game  diminishing it Gravely undermines  elements of druckman's original story  especially its Infamous climax in the  video game the player is introduced to  Ellie in a shocking yet effective way as  Joel carries a critically injured  Marlene into her apartment Ellie lunges  from behind the door with a small knife  to protect her emitting her iconic first  line  away from me  this line of dialogue is key because it  clarifies that Ellie is acting out of  concern for her friend rather than  self-preservation in this scene Neil  druckman quickly establishes Ellie as  Brave selfless and caring while implying  that Marlene is a reliable caregiver to  players who have so far experienced  nothing but selfish survivalism this  Dynamic introduces Marlene and Ellie as  refreshing figures of Hope while the  video game accomplishes all this in a  matter of seconds the series spends  roughly six minutes without revealing  much about Ellie's genuine personality  any semblance of their friendship has  been erased and without it both  characters are indistinguishable from  everyone else it's revealed shortly into  the game that Marlene has been caring  for Ellie since her mother passed away  as such it can be inferred that she's  been more or less raised on the ideology  of the fireflies though she claims not  to be an official member it's clear that  Ellie believes in what they stand for  arguably the most damaging aspect of  Marlene and Ellie's altered history for  the TV series is that Ellie no longer  has a personal stake in the Firefly's  cause without this investment it's hard  to understand why she agrees to go with  Joel when she very clearly wants to be  left to her own devices while in the  video game she fights through enemies  for a future that she has clear  unwavering and optimistic faith in she  seems to trudge along in the series  solely out of a sense of obligation it  already seems as though the writers will  eventually try to pin Ellie's conviction  on an unseen history with Marlene or  something to do with her mother in  either case it's a shame if a series  with so much screen time has to rely on  off-screen events and interactions to  motivate one of its Central protagonists  what really sets The Last of Us Apart is  that it manages to be both emotionally  arresting and dramatically surprising  all at once Neil druckman's  establishment of Marlene as a reliable  caregiver not only helps players  empathize with her as a character it  also slightly conceals the game's final  twist Marlene is immediately introduced  as a hardened revolutionary who will  risk anything for her cause like any  good Storyteller druckman lays his cards  on the table from the start but he still  manages to surprise audiences at the  story's conclusion without sacrificing  anything he's built previously druckman  humanizes his characters so thoroughly  you almost forget Extinction looms just  over the horizon Marlene is quite  refreshingly presented as a hopeful  figure in a bleak future by making her a  reliable character with a deep emotional  attachment to Ellie and seemingly just  goals players aren't primed to expect  what she'll do to see those goals  through Neil druckman recently told the  New Yorker that he views the last of us  as an exploration of the limits of the  unconditional love a parent has for  their child for the climax of this  exploration to be satisfying Joel and  Marlene need to have one thing in common  by the time they see each other again in  Salt Lake City they both need to love  Ellie like a daughter in the game it's  clear Marlene's affection for Ellie a  strong and long-standing  but whatever it is you think you're  going through right now is nothing to  what I have been through  I knew her since she was born in the  series however Ellie is essentially  Marlene's prisoner the drama of the  game's finale is derived from the  similarity in how Marlene and Joel feel  about Ellie it creates this idea that  with the right argument one of them  could realistically persuade the other  in the series since Ellie has only known  Marlene for a day or two it's hard to  imagine the writers achieving the same  level of payoff Marlene is a soldier not  a parent of course her treatment of  Ellie would differ from Joel's in this  context the conflict shifts from a  contest of family to one of ideology  despite the missing setup of Marlene and  Ellie's history it's still possible that  the HBO series could pull off a  satisfying finale even with their  lacking relationship aside from Neil  druckman and Craig Mason's  well-established talents as storytellers  and Bella Ramsay and Merle dandridge's  exceptional performances as their  respective characters the show has  proven its ability to offer something  new what they've built so far may not  work especially well for the game's  climax but perhaps it will be perfectly  suited to some new twist they've taken  for the series after all no show could  ever be a one-to-one adaptation of the  game's original story arc there's also a  possibility that future episodes will  give Marlene and Ellie's relationship  more texture giving the Final Act the  mutual heartbreak it desperately needs  [Music]
 

